5. **Prove coverage at every refinement.**  
   Each finite window needs both exhaustive enumeration inside the window and a theorem covering everything outside it.

6. **Make archive termination explicit.**  
   Require an acyclic dependency graph or a decreasing mathematical rank.

7. **Separate pure-solution checking from pure-solution completeness.**  
   Supply the argument bounding exponents or otherwise covering the infinite Diophantine equation.

8. **Adversarially test larger supports.**  
   The first question should be what happens to a hypothetical node with \(19\), \(25\), or more unrepresented support primes—not whether the stored \(18\)-slot product recomputes correctly.

If these steps succeed, a \(q\ge19\) program becomes meaningfully grounded. If they fail, enlarging the search does not address the defect.

